-- title:
--   Special fibre of J₁(Mp) as glued Pic⁰ of Igusa curves
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and an integer $M$ with $5 \le M$ and $p \nmid M$ (`hM`, `hpM`). The arithmetic base data consist of: a field $L$ of characteristic zero which is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ of type $\{p\}$, an element $\zeta \in L$ that is a primitive $p$-th root of unity (`hζ`); an intermediate field $K$ of $L \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ which by `hK` equals [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), that is, the $L$-subfield of $L((q))$ generated by the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion function field of $X_1(Mp)$ over $\mathbb{Q}$; a discrete valuation domain $A$ with fraction field $L$ such that $p$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$ (`hAp`) and $\zeta$ lies in the image of $A$ (`hζA`), together with an $A$-algebra structure on $K$ compatible with $A \to L \to K$; and an element $j \in K$, nonzero, whose Laurent series is the $q$-expansion [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157) of the modular $j$-invariant pushed into $L((q))$ (`hj`). Over $\mathrm{Spec}\,A$ one has the two-chart model [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.modelTo A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L252).
--
--   The geometry of the geometric special fibre is encoded as follows. Let $k$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$, with an $A$-algebra structure, and write $\mathfrak{X}_k$ for the base change `baseChange A (ModularCurve.TwoChart.modelTo A K j) k`, the pullback of the model along $\mathrm{Spec}\,k \to \mathrm{Spec}\,A$, viewed over $\mathrm{Spec}\,k$. Let $c_1 : C_1 \to \mathrm{Spec}\,k$ and $c_2 : C_2 \to \mathrm{Spec}\,k$ be proper, smooth of relative dimension $1$ and geometrically integral, and let $i_1, i_2$ be morphisms $C_1 \to \mathfrak{X}_k$, $C_2 \to \mathfrak{X}_k$ over $\mathrm{Spec}\,k$ that are closed immersions. The hypothesis `hcover` states that every point of the underlying space of $\mathfrak{X}_k$ lies in the image of $i_1$ or of $i_2$; `hred` states that the scheme-theoretic intersection $\mathrm{pullback}\,i_1\,i_2$ is reduced; `hn` fixes $n$ as the number of its points and `hn0` requires $n > 0$.
--
--   The sections and the representability data are: a section $\varepsilon$ of `modelTo` over $\mathrm{Spec}\,A$, sections $\varepsilon_1$ of $c_1$ and $\varepsilon_2$ of $c_2$ over $\mathrm{Spec}\,k$, with `hε₁` saying that $\varepsilon_1$ followed by $i_1$ is the base-changed section `sectionBaseChange k ε`; a relative $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ designation $D$ for `modelTo` over $A$ (a scheme with a structure morphism `D.toBase` to $\mathrm{Spec}\,A$ and a zero section), with `hrep` asserting that $D$, equipped with some Poincaré rigidified line bundle, represents the subfunctor of rigidified line bundles cut out by `algEquivZeroCut` (the fibrewise algebraic-equivalence-to-zero condition `FibrewiseAlgEquivZero`) in the sense of `RepresentsRelSubPic`: the Poincaré bundle satisfies the condition, every rigidified bundle over a base $t$ satisfying it is the pullback of the Poincaré bundle along a unique morphism over $\mathrm{Spec}\,A$, and the pullback along the zero section is trivial; `hsm` and `hsep` assert that `D.toBase` is smooth and separated. Further, `hreps` asserts that the base change `D.baseChange k` represents the corresponding subfunctor for $\mathfrak{X}_k$ with the section `sectionBaseChange k ε`, and `hPk` that the Poincaré bundle of `hreps` is isomorphic to the bundle obtained by `BaseChange.ofR` from the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hrep` along the first projection of $D.\mathrm{toBase} \times_{\mathrm{Spec}\,A} \mathrm{Spec}\,k$. Designations $D_1$ for $(C_1, \varepsilon_1)$ and $D_2$ for $(C_2, \varepsilon_2)$ are given with the analogous representability hypotheses `hrep₁`, `hrep₂`. Finally, $\nu_2$ is a morphism from the base of `D.baseChange k` to the base of $D_2$ over $\mathrm{Spec}\,k$, and `hν₂` identifies it as the restriction map to $C_2$ on the level of bundles: for every $k$-scheme $t : T \to \mathrm{Spec}\,k$ and every point $a$ of `(D.baseChange k).toBase` over $t$, the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hrep₂` along $a$ followed by $\nu_2$ is isomorphic to the rigidification, along `rigSection c₂ t ε₂` and $\mathrm{pr}_2$, of the pullback along `curveChange i₂.1 i₂.2 t` of the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hreps` along $a$.
--
--   The abstract special-fibre datum is $G$ : [`ModularCurve.JOneP.NeronSpecialFibreGeom p`](def/ModularCurve_JOnePGeom.html#L9), consisting of abelian groups $G.\mathrm{J0s}$, $G.\mathrm{JI}$, $G.\mathrm{JE}$, a subgroup $G.\mathrm{torus} \le G.\mathrm{J0s}$ and a surjective homomorphism $G.\mathrm{proj} : G.\mathrm{J0s} \to G.\mathrm{JI} \times G.\mathrm{JE}$ whose kernel is $G.\mathrm{torus}$. It is tied to the schemes by bijections `pts`, `ptsI`, `ptsE` of $G.\mathrm{J0s}$, $G.\mathrm{JI}$, $G.\mathrm{JE}$ with the sets of $k$-points (sections over the identity of $\mathrm{Spec}\,k$) of the bases of `D.baseChange k`, $D_1$, $D_2$ respectively; by the additivity hypotheses `hadd`, `haddI`, `haddE`, which say that for all $a, b$ the pullback of the relevant Poincaré bundle along the point attached to $a + b$ is isomorphic to the tensor product of the pullbacks along the points attached to $a$ and to $b$; and by `hproj`, which says that for every $x \in G.\mathrm{J0s}$ the point attached to the first component of $G.\mathrm{proj}\,x$ is `pts x` followed by the comparison morphism `RepresentsRelSubPic.pullbackHom i₁.1 i₁.2 hε₁ hreps hrep₁.some`, and the point attached to the second component is `pts x` followed by $\nu_2$.
--
--   The Igusa side consists of: $w$ : [`ModularCurve.IntegralWeightOneForm k M`](def/ModularCurve_IgusaFunctionFieldX1.html#L16), namely a weight-one modular form on $\Gamma_1(M)$ together with an integral power series realising its $q$-expansion and the requirement that the associated series over $k$ is nonzero; and two curve models $\mathrm{Mdl}_1, \mathrm{Mdl}_2$ over $k$ of the Igusa function field $F :=$ [`ModularCurve.igusaFunctionFieldX1C k M w`](def/ModularCurve_IgusaFunctionFieldX1.html#L35) (each an integral proper scheme, smooth of relative dimension $1$ over $k$, with function field identified with $F$ and closed points in bijection with the places of $F$ over $k$ in a way matching stalks with valuation rings), together with isomorphisms $e_1 : \mathrm{Mdl}_1.C \cong C_1$ and $e_2 : \mathrm{Mdl}_2.C \cong C_2$ compatible with the structure morphisms (`he₁`, `he₂`).
--
--   Under these hypotheses there exist a finite set $\mathrm{nodes}$ of pairs of places of $F$ over $k$, an additive isomorphism $\Psi : G.\mathrm{J0s} \cong \mathrm{GluedPic}^0(k, F, \mathrm{nodes})$ — where the right-hand side is the quotient of the group of admissible gluing data (pairs of degree-zero divisors together with a family of elements of $\mathrm{Additive}\,k^\times$ indexed by $\mathrm{nodes}$, the first divisor vanishing at every first coordinate and the second at every second coordinate of a node) by the subgroup of glued principal data — and additive isomorphisms $\theta_1 : G.\mathrm{JI} \cong \mathrm{Pic}^0(k, F)$, $\theta_2 : G.\mathrm{JE} \cong \mathrm{Pic}^0(k, F)$, such that all of the following hold.
--
--   First, a pair $\sigma$ of places belongs to $\mathrm{nodes}$ if and only if there is a morphism $z : \mathrm{Spec}\,k \to \mathrm{pullback}\,i_1\,i_2$ such that $z$ followed by the first projection and then $c_1$ is the identity, $z$ followed by the second projection and then $c_2$ is the identity, and $\sigma$ is the pair of places attached by `Mdl₁.pointEquivPlace` and `Mdl₂.pointEquivPlace` to the resulting $k$-points $z \circ \mathrm{pr}_1$ of $\mathrm{Mdl}_1.C$ and $z \circ \mathrm{pr}_2$ of $\mathrm{Mdl}_2.C$ (transported through $e_1^{-1}$, $e_2^{-1}$).
--
--   Second, $\mathrm{nodes}$ has cardinality $n$. Third, the first-coordinate projection is injective on $\mathrm{nodes}$, and fourth, so is the second-coordinate projection.
--
--   Fifth, for every $x \in G.\mathrm{J0s}$ the pair of degree-zero classes obtained from $\Psi x$ by `GluedPic0.toPic0Pair` (the map sending the class of a gluing datum to the pair of classes of its two divisors) equals $(\theta_1((G.\mathrm{proj}\,x)_1), \theta_2((G.\mathrm{proj}\,x)_2))$.
--
--   Sixth, the image of $G.\mathrm{torus}$ under $\Psi$ is exactly the range of `GluedPic0.nodeUnit`, the homomorphism sending a family of units indexed by $\mathrm{nodes}$ to the class of the gluing datum with both divisors zero.
--
--   Seventh, for every $g \in G.\mathrm{JI}$ and every $k$-point $x$ of $C_1$: if the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hrep₁` along the point `ptsI g` is isomorphic to the tensor product of the line bundle of the relative effective Cartier divisor `RelEffCartierDiv.ofPoint c₁ x` with the ideal module of `RelEffCartierDiv.ofPoint c₁ ε₁`, then there is a degree-zero divisor $Dv$ on $F$ equal to the difference of the delta-divisors at the places attached by `Mdl₁.pointEquivPlace` to $x$ and to $\varepsilon_1$ (transported through $e_1^{-1}$), each with multiplicity $1$, and $\theta_1 g$ is the class of $Dv$.
--
--   Eighth, the same statement with $C_2$, $\varepsilon_2$, `hrep₂`, `ptsE`, $\mathrm{Mdl}_2$, $e_2$ and $\theta_2$ in place of their index-one counterparts.
--
--   This is Raynaud's description of $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of a curve whose geometric special fibre is the union of two smooth components meeting transversally, specialised to the stable model of $X_1(Mp)$ at $p$ and to the two Igusa components: the group of $k$-points of the special fibre of $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ is read as glued degree-zero divisor classes on the Igusa function field, the toric part corresponding to the gluing units at the nodes and the two projections to the degree-zero Picard groups of the components. It is the passage from the scheme-theoretic special-fibre datum to divisor-class language, and is used downstream in the analysis of the component and toric parts of $J_1(Mp)$ at $p$, in particular by the statements on $n$-divisibility of point classes and on the Hecke action on the special fibre.
